### Architecture assessment

The module-driven structure is sound and repeatedly proven: surfaces compose through injected runners; `web -> app` and runcontrol-leaf boundaries are machine-enforced; the change-06 probe ("add review/smoke strategy") found the edit surface matches expectations in all five scenarios; `platform/runtime` is earned boundary translation; `internal/app` performs real projection/fingerprint/classification work, not forwarding. Roadmap fences themselves are unusually precise (Sprint 35 may pick operational storage; product-artifact persistence is Gate D).

The stress is concentrated where policy was copied instead of owned: (1) failure-persistence decisions live per hand-copied branch in seven flow functions and have already diverged (F01); (2) ordered stage vocabulary is re-enumerated by hand in ~12 lists outside `sprint.PlanningStages()` with no parity test, and the newest stage escaped four of them (F05, F08); (3) the observability payload vocabulary has three producer-side promotion layers but a frozen storage allowlist that deletes exactly what the newest commits promote (F02); (4) implementation moved product state into SQLite ahead of the recorded authority decision, silently co-tenanting the operational database (F03, F04); (5) doc contracts pinned by tests describe an older vocabulary than the code emits (F07).

### Candidate findings

#### CHALLENGE-06-F01
- **Priority**: P1
- **Claim**: Two branches of the planning-flow template persist `flow-state.json` during dry runs — unlike every sibling branch — so a failing preview mutates product state without holding the mutation lease, contradicting the CLI contract, the web confirmation text, and this repo's own regression tests.
- **Evidence**: `internal/sprint/service.go` `FlowTechnicalHandbook`: the "selected evidence validation failed" branch calls `_ = SaveFlowState(s.root, sp, NewFlowState(sp, stages, now))` with no `if !req.DryRun` guard, while adjacent branches in the same function (requirements placeholder, prompt-compose error) guard it. Same asymmetry in `FlowReasoning`: requirements-placeholder branch unguarded, prerequisites-failed branch guarded. Dry runs skip lease acquisition: `flow.go:117-131` (`FlowStage` acquires mutation context only `if !req.DryRun`). Reachable from browser/TUI: `app/operations.go:340` `OperationStageDryRun -> ss.FlowStage(..., DryRun: true)`; confirmation says "runtime-free; no runtime-backed writes" (`app/operations.go:180`) while `OperationSprintStatus` explicitly warns "MAY REFRESH FLOW-STATE.JSON" — the contract distinguishes these and the dry-run warning omits the write. Contract: `docs/cli-reference.md` ("--dry-run ... makes no writes"). Tests pin the guarded branches only: `handbook_test.go:71` and `reasoning_test.go:150` assert no `flow-state.json` after dry runs; no test exercises the two unguarded branches under dry run.
- **Architectural reason**: failure-semantics (+ drift): persist-on-failure policy is re-decided per copied branch instead of owned once by the Flow driver; 56 `SaveFlowState` call sites exist in `service.go` alone.
- **Concrete consequence**: With a real run active (lease held), an operator previewing technical-handbook/reasoning triggers a whole-file replace from a stale snapshot with no lease, reverting the lease holder's recorded stage progress; on the read-only serve surface it happens while the UI promises no writes.
- **Counter-evidence searched**: No test/doc records intent that failing dry runs record state; checked whether `WithoutStatusWrites` gates these writes (it gates `Status()` only); checked whether dry runs hold the lease (they do not); checked git history via specialist-23a (unguarded lines predate the guard convention).
- **Confidence**: high
- **Smallest useful action**: Add `if !req.DryRun` guards at the two unguarded branches to match siblings, plus one regression test per branch asserting no `flow-state.json` after a failing dry run. Optionally consolidate the persist-on-failure decision into one driver-level helper (see F05 remedy pattern) if touch budget allows.

#### CHALLENGE-06-F02
- **Priority**: P1
- **Claim**: The durable event pipeline promotes observability payload keys at two producer layers, but the runcontrol storage allowlist still contains none of them — the feature shipped by commits `c455510`/`eeaa034` is deleted at the storage gate, and replayed timelines show omission noise where live SSE shows detail.
- **Evidence**: Producer layer A: `app/run_control.go:435` promote map `{tool,title,detail,text,delta,content,message,action,state,status,native_type,line}`; force-extraction loop `run_control.go:479-486`. Producer layer B: `platform/runtime/runtime.go:592-604` (per specialist-24b; consistent with commit eeaa034 message). Storage gate: `runcontrol/sanitize.go:10-17` allowlist contains only `{runtime_event_id,runtime_run_id,agentwrap_run_id,session_id,external_harness_run_id,type,kind,state,status,outcome,reason,category,code,count,known,input_tokens,output_tokens,total_tokens,turns,artifact_kind,artifact_id,lifecycle,liveness,message,index,tool,action}` — no `title,detail,text,delta,content,output,phase,name,native_type,line`; applied on every append (`sqlite.go` sanitize call path). Consumer reads the dropped keys: `web/run_handlers.go:291` `firstNonEmptyPayload(...,"text","delta","detail","message","content","title","output")` — only `message` survives. Live path differs: `web/operations.go:264` renders `"detail"` from `OperationEvent` directly. Each dropped event accrues `Omission{Reason:"unsafe event detail omitted"}` (`sanitize.go:29-45`).
- **Architectural reason**: boundary + change-surface: runcontrol owns the vocabulary gate but nothing ties it to producers/consumers — no shared schema artifact, no round-trip test.
- **Concrete consequence**: Replay/history views lose exactly the fields the last two observability commits added while each event gains an "Omitted N detail item(s)" row; live and replay timelines disagree; every future observable field requires synchronized edits in >=3 packages with zero compile/test protection.
- **Counter-evidence searched**: change-04 defends the allowlist as a deliberate single sanitization gate — valid for security scoping, but it does not explain promoting keys upstream that the gate then deletes; searched for a second unsanitized durable read path (none per scout-05/24b); searched for a test asserting end-to-end survival of promoted keys (none; `run_handlers_test.go` fixtures carry only `kind/type/tool`).
- **Confidence**: high
- **Smallest useful action**: Decide the owner of promotion (recommend platform/runtime; delete the duplicate map in `run_control.go`), extend `allowedEventPayloadFields` to the promoted set, add one round-trip test: promoted producer key survives Append->Events->`newRunEventView`.

